WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:04.880
Pablo Larraín is an
incredible creative mind.

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:07.633
Pablo is such an artist.

00:00:09.176 --> 00:00:10.135
He's a visionary.

00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:12.721
I come from the world of cinema,

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:14.848
where things are condensed to two hours.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:20.187
Here the invitation was to do
an eight-hour episodic television show.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:25.484
Stephen King is someone
who's been able to

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:30.531
understand our humanity
in a very universal way.

00:00:32.491 --> 00:00:35.619
The last 50 years of our culture

00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:39.790
have been somehow shaped
by his stories.

00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:48.590
I went to Maine, and I stayed
at Stephen King's house,

00:00:48.966 --> 00:00:50.801
which was a very fascinating experience.

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:55.138
Reading and kind of
 going over the scripts,

00:00:55.222 --> 00:00:59.476
trying for me to understand
what he really was looking for,

00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:01.436
that maybe was not exactly on the page.

00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:04.731
I personally struggled in the beginning.

00:01:04.815 --> 00:01:08.068
I wanted to take everything
to a more grounded sort of space,

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:11.822
where the narrative was more
naturalistic or realistic.

00:01:13.115 --> 00:01:16.994
And he was like,
"No, you have to come to my world,

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.246
where all the things happen
to the characters.

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:23.083
They do travel to different worlds
and different realities."

00:01:25.544 --> 00:01:27.880
So I had to go, as a filmmaker,

00:01:28.005 --> 00:01:30.174
through a process
of embrace that narrative.

00:01:31.884 --> 00:01:33.552
And it's funny, because it's somehow

00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:35.596
what happens to
the main character as well.

00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:40.184
Lisey's Story is a story of a woman
who is trying to put herself together,

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.603
to move on with her life.

00:01:42.978 --> 00:01:45.522
Lisey doesn't want to admit
that that world exists,

00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:47.316
or that
that kind of things can happen.

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:50.068
It's wonderful for me as a filmmaker,

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:54.198
because you have to find a way
to create certain layers

00:01:54.406 --> 00:01:57.784
of a narrative that is placed 
in this fragmented memory.

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:02.748
Memories combine and mix with fantasy.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.794
And that fantasy goes into
the real world, and vice versa.

00:02:08.921 --> 00:02:10.506
It was just a fascinating process,

00:02:11.423 --> 00:02:15.844
to embrace the fantasies
and the psychological horror

00:02:15.928 --> 00:02:18.180
in a way that has
some poetry and some beauty.

00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:23.227
This journey of humanity

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:28.232
will take us to
pain, sorrow, beauty, family, love...

00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:30.901
a lot of darkness,

00:02:31.443 --> 00:02:33.529
and a lot of light and healing.

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:36.490
The experience of doing this,

00:02:36.615 --> 00:02:39.159
it really changed me and
made me a different filmmaker.

00:02:39.243 --> 00:02:40.410
So I'm very grateful.

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:43.372
Why are they so important to you?

00:02:44.748 --> 00:02:46.875
Because stories are all I have.

